  <section class="content-section" id="section-core-concepts">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Tricks, Sets, and Modifiers</h2></div>
    <p>
      Freestyle has its own words for the moves: the surfaces you catch the
      bag on, the leg circles and body turns that shape a trick, and the
      families tricks grow into. Small movements combine into named tricks,
      and every named trick is in the
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks">trick dictionary</a>.
    </p>

    {{!-- Single-authority rule: a modifier's ADD lives in the modifier registry, and
          what each operator is, its structure and its X-Dex behaviour, in the
          freestyle_operators table. Any operator/modifier wording on this page is
          secondary and must stay consistent with them; edit them first, then
          reconcile here. --}}
    <h3 id="tricks-sets-modifiers">Tricks, sets, and modifiers</h3>
    <p>
      Three kinds of named thing make up the vocabulary. They are related but
      not interchangeable.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt>Trick</dt>
      <dd>
        A recognized movement pattern that resolves to a defined outcome, usually
        a stall or kick (Mirage, Butterfly, Osis, Torque). A small number resolve
        through a swing element instead (Pendulum, Rake). Tricks are catalogued in
        the <a href="/freestyle/tricks">trick dictionary</a>.
      </dd>
      <dt>Set</dt>
      <dd>
        An entry mechanism that launches into another movement (Pixie, Fairy,
        Stepping, Atomic, Quantum). A set initiates movement rather than ending
        it, so it becomes a trick only when combined with a terminal outcome such
        as a stall or kick (Pixie Set &rarr; Pixie, Fairy Set &rarr; Fairy). That
        is why one word can name both a set and a trick. Sets have their own
        <a href="/freestyle/sets">Set Encyclopedia</a>.
      </dd>
      <dt>Modifier</dt>
      <dd>
        A transformation applied to an existing trick, not normally performed on
        its own (Spinning, Ducking, Symposium, Paradox). The one-line
        tell: <strong>Spinning is a modifier; Spin is a trick.</strong> Modifiers
        live on <a href="/freestyle/operators">Operators &amp; Modifiers</a> and in
        the <a href="#section-modifiers">modifiers section</a> below.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="detail-muted">
      The <a href="/freestyle/glossary">Glossary</a> gives each term a short
      definition; this page explains how the terms fit together.
    </p>

    {{#if content.foundationalMoves.length}}
    <h3 id="core-trick-atoms">Twelve foundational moves</h3>
    <p>
      Two resting stalls and ten basic moves are the foundation the
      dictionary teaches from, though not every trick breaks down into them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {{#each content.foundationalMoves}}
      <li id="term-{{slug}}"><a href="{{siteHref href}}">{{name}}</a>{{#if addLabel}} ({{addLabel}}){{/if}}</li>
      {{/each}}
    </ul>
    {{/if}}

    <p id="section-families">
      Tricks that share an ending form a family. The
      <a href="{{siteHref content.familiesHref}}">family list</a> shows each
      family and the tricks in it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-surfaces">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Contact Surfaces and Stalls</h2></div>
    <p>
      A <strong>contact surface</strong> is where the foot or body meets the
      bag. A <strong>stall</strong> holds the bag
      on a surface; a <strong>kick</strong> redirects it. Most stalls are
      1 ADD.
    </p>

    <h3>Foundational surfaces</h3>
    <p>The toe and the clipper anchor most named tricks.</p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-toe">Toe</dt>
      <dd>Top of the shoe, near the toe-box. The default surface for sets and many stalls. Often abbreviated <code>TOE</code> in operational notation.</dd>
      <dt id="term-clipper">Clipper</dt>
      <dd>Clipper is a cross-body contact surface. The canonical Clipper Stall reads <code>SET &gt; OP CLIP [XBD] [DEL]</code>; its SAME/OP relation is component-relative and is not fixed merely by the contact surface. Often abbreviated <code>CLIP</code>.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Foot surfaces</h3>
    <p>Less common as catch surfaces; appear regularly in specialized stalls and named compounds.</p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-inside">Inside</dt>
      <dd>The inner side of the foot. Used in inside stalls and as a transitional contact in compounds that travel across the body.</dd>
      <dt id="term-outside">Outside</dt>
      <dd>The outer side of the foot. Used in outside stalls; pairs with inside in same-foot transitions.</dd>
      <dt id="term-sole">Sole</dt>
      <dd>Bottom of the shoe. Stylistic and specialized; the surface for sole stalls and a number of cross-body variants.</dd>
      <dt id="term-heel">Heel</dt>
      <dd>Back of the shoe. Rare as a catch surface; appears in stylistic stalls and certain trick variants.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Body-region surfaces</h3>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-knee">Knee</dt>
      <dd>Top of the knee. Used in knee stalls and knee-balanced positions during routines.</dd>
      <dt id="term-shin">Shin</dt>
      <dd>Front of the lower leg. Surface for shin stalls, which the dictionary names <a href="#term-cloud">cloud stalls</a>.</dd>
      <dt id="term-neck">Neck</dt>
      <dd>Back-of-neck stall. Multiple variants distinguished by which side the bag enters and how the head tilts; most appear in routine choreography.</dd>
      <dt id="term-shoulder">Shoulder</dt>
      <dd>Top of the shoulder. Stylistic; appears in routine work and stall-chaining sequences.</dd>
      <dt id="term-chest">Chest</dt>
      <dd>Upper-chest stall. Stylistic and routine-oriented.</dd>
      <dt id="term-back">Back</dt>
      <dd>Upper-back stall. Specialized and rare; appears in advanced freestyle routines.</dd>
      <dt id="term-head">Head</dt>
      <dd>Top-of-head stall. Stylistic; appears in head-balance sequences and routine work.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Specialized variants &amp; alternative names</h3>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-flapper">Flapper</dt>
      <dd>A cross-body sole stall (<code>XBD</code> + sole). The community name for the cross-body variant of a sole stall.</dd>
      <dt id="term-cloud">Cloud (cloud stall)</dt>
      <dd>The dictionary's name for a shin stall: &ldquo;cloud&rdquo; is the surface label on trick pages, and &ldquo;shin stall&rdquo; the descriptive form.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-dexterities">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Dexterities</h2></div>
    <p>
      A <strong>dex</strong> (dexterity) is a motion where the foot circles
      around the bag. The dex is the heart of freestyle; almost every
      named compound trick contains at least one.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Mirage uses one dex.</li>
      <li>Double Around the World uses two dexes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Direction</h3>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-in-dex">In-dex</dt>
      <dd>A dex that travels <em>inward</em>: the bag arcs toward the body's center line. Paired with in-direction set primitives (pixie, quantum) and visible in compounds like <a href="/freestyle/tricks/dimwalk">Dimwalk</a> (pixie butterfly) and Toe Blur (<a href="/freestyle/tricks/quantum_mirage">quantum mirage</a>).</dd>

      <dt id="term-out-dex">Out-dex</dt>
      <dd>A dex that travels <em>outward</em>: the bag arcs away from the body's center line. Paired with out-direction set primitives (atomic, fairy) and visible in compounds like Atom Smasher (atomic mirage).</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Relative-side relationships</h3>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-same-side"><code>SAME</code> (same-side)</dt>
      <dd>The dex acts on the same leg as the most recent side-bearing component (abbreviated <code>ss</code>). It is not implied by a clipper or toe entry.</dd>

      <dt id="term-opposite-side"><code>OP</code> (opposite)</dt>
      <dd>The dex acts on the opposite leg from the most recent side-bearing component (abbreviated <code>op</code>). <code>SAME</code> and <code>OP</code> are different executions even when they score the same.</dd>

      <dt id="term-paradox-side">Paradox (<code>PDX</code>)</dt>
      <dd>Paradox marks a distinct relationship on a dexterity. It is not a third SAME/OP value; <code>[PDX]</code> can coexist with <code>SAME</code> or <code>OP</code>, and independently with <code>[XDEX]</code>.</dd>

      <dt id="term-op-not-xdex"><code>OP</code> is not X-Dex</dt>
      <dd><code>OP</code> is a component-relative leg relation: the dex acts on the opposite leg from the most recent side-bearing component. Near and far are other names for these relations: near is <code>SAME</code>, far is <code>OP</code>. Neither adds difficulty on its own. <a href="#term-x-dex">X-Dex</a> is a separate +1, scored only where the operational notation carries <code>[XDEX]</code>.</dd>

      <dt id="term-x-dex">X-Dex</dt>
      <dd>An extra dexterity worth +1. It is scored wherever the operational notation carries the <code>[XDEX]</code> marker on a dex; the notation is the source of truth. For example, the <a href="/freestyle/tricks/atom_smasher">Atom Smasher</a> carries <code>[XDEX]</code> and scores the extra +1, while <a href="/freestyle/tricks/atomic_miraging_butterfly">Atomic Miraging Butterfly</a> does not. X-Dex is its own component: never part of a modifier's own weight, and never assumed from an operator name.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-timing-sets">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Timing and Sets</h2></div>
    <p>
      A trick has a clock. The <strong>set</strong> positions the bag; the
      bag rises, peaks, falls; the trick catches. Modifiers (see the
      Operators &amp; Modifiers section, below) are classified by which
      beat they transform.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      <dt id="term-set">Set</dt>
      <dd>The launch action that begins the trick and positions the bag. A dex performed as part of that launch has a set role; the same movement performed after the launch, before the final catch, is a standalone realization. Which role a trick uses follows its established structure and execution, not a single timing instant.</dd>
      <dt id="term-set-realization">Set realization</dt>
      <dd>A dex performed as part of launching and positioning the bag. It can terminate immediately on a toe and still be a genuine set; a following component makes the role easier to recognize but is not required. Realized as sets, the inward and outward movements are named Quantum and Atomic.</dd>
      <dt id="term-standalone-realization">Standalone realization</dt>
      <dd>A dex performed on its own once the bag is launched, before the final catch, rather than as part of the launch: the inward and outward standalone tricks are Mirage and Illusion. Miraging and Illusioning describe those movements in established names such as Miraging Kick and Illusioning Kick, but they are not reusable scored operators and do not license arbitrary compounds.</dd>
      <dt id="term-uptime">Uptime</dt>
      <dd>Before the peak. The bag is rising.</dd>
      <dt id="term-midtime">Midtime (hangtime)</dt>
      <dd>At the peak. The brief no-plant moment.</dd>
      <dt id="term-downtime">Downtime</dt>
      <dd>After the peak. The bag is falling toward the catch.</dd>
      <dt id="term-attack">Attack</dt>
      <dd>How quickly a player begins the next phase's component. A transition that has to fire aggressively (like attacking the mirage quickly after the stepping set in Blur) is said to need a fast attack.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>How tricks begin</h3>
    <p>
      Two set surfaces dominate how tricks begin: toe and clipper.
      The launch sets Pixie, Fairy, and Stepping recur widely, with operators
      such as Paradox and Symposium layered alongside them. The set surface
      itself is too broad to be a system. The
      <a href="/freestyle/sets">Set Encyclopedia</a> describes each named set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-modifiers">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Operators and Modifiers</h2></div>
    <p>
      Modifiers form <strong>ecosystems</strong>. A single modifier transforms
      <em>many</em> different base tricks: body and rotational ecosystems
      (ducking, gyro, spinning) reshape the midtime. The named sets are defined
      in <a href="#section-timing-sets">Timing &amp; Sets</a>; several also
      broadcast across other bases as set modifiers, which is why pixie appears
      across pixie-illusion, pixie-mobius, and symposium-pixie rather than as a
      family of its own. An ecosystem is
      <strong>not a family</strong>: it has no terminal mechanic of its own; it
      modifies a base that does. Browse a launch-set ecosystem under
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks?view=set">By set</a> and a body or timing
      ecosystem under <a href="/freestyle/tricks?view=modifier">By modifier</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="lineage-or-ecosystem">Lineage or ecosystem? A test you can apply</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>lineage</strong> (a <a href="{{siteHref content.familiesHref}}">family</a> or a
      descendant lineage) has an ending of its own; an <strong>ecosystem</strong>
      is a transformation you apply to someone else&rsquo;s ending. One quick
      test settles most cases: <em>can you do it on its own?</em> You can do
      &ldquo;a whirl&rdquo; or &ldquo;an osis&rdquo;; they land. You cannot
      do &ldquo;a ducking,&rdquo; only a <em>ducking-something</em> (ducking-osis,
      ducking-mirage, ducking-clipper).
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="action-link" href="/freestyle/operators">See Every Operator and Modifier</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-notation">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>How Tricks Are Written and Scored</h2></div>
    <p class="glossary-operator-notation-framing">
      Operational notation is the compact symbolic shorthand for trick
      composition. <em>Paradox mirage</em>, for example, reads
      <code class="notation-display-tokens"><span class="notation-token">CLIP</span> &gt; <span class="notation-token">OP IN</span> <span class="notation-token">[PDX]</span> <span class="notation-token">[DEX]</span> &gt; <span class="notation-token">OP TOE</span> <span class="notation-token">[DEL]</span></code>:
      a clipper set, an opposite-leg in-out dex carrying the paradox marker, and
      a toe catch. The notation lets readers reconstruct the body mechanics from
      the name without memorizing each compound. For the full operational
      grammar and worked decompositions see
      <a href="/freestyle/add-analysis">ADD Accounting &amp; Analysis</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="compositional-premise">The compositional premise: every trick as formula</h3>
    <p>
      The notation system descends from grammar work originating on the
      footbag discussion list in 1995, which proposed that every
      freestyle trick can be expressed as a small grammar of set-type,
      side, direction, and dexterity tokens. The base shape is set out
      under <a href="#jobs-notation">Jobs notation</a> below.
    </p>

    <h3 id="jobs-notation">Jobs notation</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Jobs notation</strong> is the original compositional grammar. Its
      source article, the authoritative historical source for the
      grammar, sets out its core:
    </p>
    <pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="jobs-notation"><code class="font-mono">(toe | clip) &gt;
[(same | op)(in | out) dexterity]* &gt;
(same | op)(toe | clip)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Read informally: a trick starts with a set surface (toe or clip),
      contains zero or more dex events with same/op side and in/out
      direction, and terminates with a catch surface. The original
      article notes that <em>same</em>, <em>op</em>, and <em>no plant</em>
      are all relative to the leg previously referred to.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      Source: <em>By the Way, Not the Name</em>, message to the
      footbag.org mailing list, October 31, 1995.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      <a class="action-link" href="/freestyle/notation-article">To See the Entire Article, Press Here.</a>
    </p>

    <div id="section-add-accounting">
    <h3 id="add-philosophy">ADD philosophy</h3>
    <p>
      ADD is <em>additive structural accounting</em>: a trick's total is the
      sum of its base weight and each modifier's weight. The scored
      bracket-flags in operational notation are
      <code>[DEX]</code> / <code>[BOD]</code> / <code>[XBD]</code> /
      <code>[PDX]</code> / <code>[DEL]</code> / <code>[UNS]</code> /
      <code>[XDEX]</code> (the last scored only where the notation carries it);
      <code>[KICK]</code> marks a non-scoring kick. The same additive logic lets
      the dictionary read <em><a href="/freestyle/tricks/mobius">mobius</a></em>
      as &ldquo;gyro torque&rdquo; and arrive at the same total either way.
    </p>

    <h3>Additive formulas</h3>
    <p>
      ADD = base + modifier stack: <code>paradox(+1) + mirage(2) = 3 ADD</code>,
      and the operational notation chain mirrors the formula's term order.
      Multi-modifier compounds walk the chain outward from the base:
      <em>stepping paradox whirl</em> =
      <code>stepping(+1) + paradox(+1) + whirl(3) = 5 ADD</code>, the same chain
      that reads as the trick's composition on its detail page. Folk labels for
      two-operator stacks are decomposed into their operators so the arithmetic
      surfaces: <code>blurry = stepping + paradox</code> (see
      <a href="/freestyle/tricks/blur">Blur</a>).
    </p>

    <p>
      For the full mechanical walkthrough with all resolved formulas,
      see <a href="/freestyle/add-analysis">ADD Accounting &amp; Analysis</a>.
    </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-composition">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Runs and Sequences</h2></div>
    <p>
      Above the single-trick level, tricks combine into longer sequences. The
      vocabulary below describes how tricks are <em>used</em>, not how they
      are defined.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Run</strong>: a continuous sequence of tricks.</li>
      <li><strong>Shuffle</strong>: alternating sides rhythmically.</li>
      <li><strong>Link / Transition</strong>: the connection between tricks.</li>
      <li><strong>Connector Trick</strong>: a trick commonly used to maintain flow. Whirl-family tricks often act as connectors.</li>
      <li><strong>Dropless</strong>: a run without drops.</li>
      <li><strong>Density</strong>: average ADD per trick in a run.</li>
      <li><strong>Shred Circle</strong>: a group format where players take turns performing runs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="run-quality">Run-quality ladder</h3>
    <p>
      A run-quality tier describes a whole run, not a single trick. Each tier
      asks every trick in the run to clear a minimum ADD, so the
      <strong>lowest-ADD trick in the run sets the tier</strong>: one easy trick
      caps the whole run.
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-term-list">
      {{#each content.runQualityLadder}}
      <dt>{{term}}</dt>
      <dd>{{definition}}</dd>
      {{/each}}
    </dl>
    <p>
      Example: a run of ADD 5, 3, and 6 tricks is <strong>Guiltless</strong>
      (every trick reaches at least 3), not Fearless, because the ADD-3 trick is
      the floor.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Genuine</strong> and <strong>BOP</strong> are related terms, not
      rungs of this numeric ladder: Genuine is Guiltless excluding BOP tricks,
      and BOP is the Butterfly, Osis, and Paradox Mirage set.
    </p>

    <p id="section-run-architecture">
      The full run-architecture reference (format specifications, combo-flow
      analysis, dropless/dropful classification) is
      <a href="/freestyle/combo-analysis">Combo Analysis</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="section-sources">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Sources</h2></div>
    <p class="glossary-about-framing">
      This reference records vocabulary the footbag community built informally over four decades. Authoritative trick-level definitions live in the <a href="/freestyle/tricks">Trick Dictionary</a>; the source families below contribute to the language documented here.
    </p>
    <ul class="glossary-sources-list">
      <li><strong>Footbag.org</strong>: the long-running community archive; trick lists, event records, and the foundational vocabulary catalogued there. See also the historical per-trick move list.</li>
      <li><strong>PassBack</strong>: conceptual tutorial series which introduced and formalised much of the modern compositional vocabulary (set / modifier / body operator framings).</li>
      <li><strong>Stanford shorthand</strong>: compact symbolic notation, an alternative encoding of the compositional grammar that exposes structural symmetries the spelled-out names sometimes obscure.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.footbagmoves.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FootbagMoves</a></strong>: observational catalogue of named tricks, alternate names, and rotational variants; informs the symbolic-grammar layer of this reference.</li>
      <li><strong>AnzTrikz</strong>: Australian / New Zealand demonstration series; canonical references for many compound rotational variants.</li>
      <li><strong>Footbag Finland</strong>: Finnish freestyle demonstration footage; visual references for several dictionary entries.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="glossary-about-framing">
      <strong>Where authority comes from.</strong> These sources do not carry
      equal weight. When they conflict, the platform resolves in a fixed order:
      a curator ruling first; then the structural grammar, where a trick's
      difficulty is what its movement composition adds up to; then community
      usage, which decides the displayed name; and last an external catalogue's
      recorded number. An external source is strong evidence that a trick exists
      and is performed, but it does not override how a trick decomposes or what
      its structural ADD is.
    </p>
    <p class="glossary-about-framing">
      The vocabulary documented here rests on decades of foundational work
      by many people across the community, beginning with the first written notation;
      their contributions to freestyle's notation, archives, and movement
      language have been indispensable to this reference and the dictionary it
      supports.
    </p>
  </section>

